function engineGuide(m){const common=`# Pulse → ${m.targetEngine==='gool'?'gool 0.82.6':'Godot native audio'}\n\nTarget project: Godot 4.7 (user supplied target; not tested in that runtime).\n\n## Put these files in your project\n1. Unzip this package in Finder.\n2. Copy its contents into ${m.suggestedProjectFolder} in your Godot project. The res:// prefix means the project root, not a system folder.\n3. Keep manifest.json and loop-plan.json alongside the WAVs. The original source track is not included.\n4. Audition each imported loop and test transitions on the target machine.\n\n${m.effectsBaked?'Section EQ and gain are baked into these WAVs. Do not apply the effect settings twice.':'These WAVs are raw. The manifest contains the EQ and gain to implement in your runtime.'}\n\nLoop body WAVs contain exactly bodyFrames PCM frames. All Pulse end positions are EXCLUSIVE. Handles WAVs contain extra audio outside the loop and are not intended to loop as whole files.\n\nThis is an asset export, not an installed plugin, imported Godot resource, or a completed music runtime.\n\n`;
if(m.targetEngine==='gool')return common+`## gool 0.82.6 integration findings\nInspected from the supplied gool-0.82.6.zip, specifically godot/addons/gool/resources/gool_music_state.gd and prefabs/music_state_controller.gd.\n\n- Register each body WAV as a non-spatialized music sound. Set its looping property for loop roles; leave it off for one-shots.\n- GoolSoundBank stores named AudioStream resources. GoolMusicState stores name, sound_name and fade_ms; MusicStateController exposes add_state(name, sound_name, fade_ms) and set_state(name).\n- The controller starts transitions immediately. It explicitly does NOT implement beat/bar quantization. Do not silently reinterpret exported beat/bar/end rules as immediate transitions.\n- Crossfades use equal-power curves. Pulse supports equal-power audition, but linear rules are not equivalent.\n- A destination state has one fade duration; per-source/destination rules need an adapter.\n- Multiple selected clips with the same event label are variants, not an automatically assembled state. Assign unique state IDs or write an explicit selection policy.\n- Pickup and tail overlaps, scheduled exits and tempo control need runtime support beyond this controller.\n\nMac compatibility is unverified. The archive’s top-level README lists macOS arm64 prebuilt support; it does not establish Intel support. Some subdirectory setup documentation is older and contradictory. Build and test the matching extension against your actual Godot version before choosing this path.\n`;
return common+`## Godot native audio setup\n- Select each body WAV in Godot’s FileSystem dock. Preserve its sample rate and length: disable trimming and normalization, and avoid sample-rate conversion if using exported sample positions.\n- For loop roles, use forward looping from the first sample through the full body. Godot import Loop End -1 means end of file; this avoids translating an end-inclusive versus end-exclusive boundary by hand. One-shots should have looping disabled.\n- Use AudioStreamPlayer for non-positional music. Route through a Music bus. Do not restart loops using the finished signal or a game-frame timer.\n- AudioStreamInteractive supports clip transition rules, but the BPM/beat metadata must exist on a compatible stream. Standard WAV assets alone do not provide the same BPM metadata controls as Ogg Vorbis/MP3. Do not assume beat/bar transitions will work just because manifest.json contains BPM.\n- For beat-aware playback, implement a verified adapter or convert reviewed bodies to an appropriate stream format and set tempo metadata in Godot. Retest length and seams after conversion.\n- manifest.json describes the intended behavior; it is not imported automatically.\n\nReferences: https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_audiostreamwav.html and https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_audiostreaminteractive.html\n`;
}

